ound each matching object while preserving enclosed holes.\n  10: \n  11:     Input structure:\n  12:     - The grid has a dominant background color.\n  13:     - One or more two-row markers sit on the left edge; each marker is a 2x2 block whose left column is an object color and right column is its halo color.\n  14:     - Away from the markers, every source color from the legend appears as one or more 4-connected monochrome objects.\n  15: \n  16:     Rule:\n  17:     - Keep the input pixels unchanged.\n  18:     - For each source-color object, take its tight bbox expanded by one cell and clipped to the grid.\n  19:     - Recolor background pixels in that expanded region to the marker's halo color, except for background holes enclosed by the object.\n  20: \n  21:     Key insight:\n  22:     - The small left-edge bars are a legend: they map object colors to the colors used for the surrounding padded bbox.\n  23:     \"\"\"\n  24:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n  25:     bg = int(get_bg(g))\n  26:     out = g.copy()\n  27:     h, w = g.shape\n  28: \n  29:     markers: dict[int, int] = {}\n  30:     marker_source_cells: dict[int, set[tuple[int, int]]] = {}\n  31:     for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=False, return_format=\"dict\"):\n  32:         r0, c0, r1, c1 = comp[\"bbox\"]\n  33:         height = int(r1 - r0 + 1)\n  34:         if c0 != 0 or c1 != 1 or height != 2 or int(comp[\"area\"]) != 4:\n  35:             continue\n  36: \n  37:         sub = g[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]\n  38:         assert np.all(sub != bg), \"legend marker must be a full 2x2 foreground block\"\n  39:         assert np.all(sub[:, 0] == sub[0, 0]), \"legend source column must be monochrome\"\n  40:         assert np.all(sub[:, 1] == sub[0, 1]), \"legend halo column must be monochrome\"\n  41:         source = int(sub[0, 0])\n  42:         halo = int(sub[0, 1])\n  43:         assert source != halo, \"legend source and halo colors must differ\"\n  44:         assert source not in markers, \"each source color must have exactly one legend marker\"\n  45: \n  46:         markers[source] = halo\n  47:         marker_source_cells[source] = {tuple(map(int, coord)) for coord in comp[\"coords\"] if int(g[tuple(coord)]) == source}\n  48: \n  49:     assert markers, \"expected at least one left-edge legend marker\"\n  50: \n  51:     for source, halo in markers.items():\n  52:         objects = []\n  53:         for comp in find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True, colors=[source], return_format=\"dict\"):\n  54:             cells = {tuple(map(int, coord)) for coord in comp[\"coords\"]}\n  55:             if cells <= marker_source_cells[source]:\n  56:                 continue\n  57:             objects.append(comp)\n  58:         assert objects, \"each legend source color must label at least one non-marker object\"\n  59: \n  60:         for obj in objects:\n  61:             r0, c0, r1, c1 = obj[\"bbox\"]\n  62:             pr0 = max(0, int(r0) - 1)\n  63:             pc0 = max(0, int(c0) - 1)\n  64:             pr1 = min(h - 1, int(r1) + 1)\n  65:             pc1 = min(w - 1, int(c1) + 1)\n  66: \n  67:             patch = np.full((pr1 - pr0 + 1, pc1 - pc0 + 1), bg, dtype=int)\n  68:             coords = obj[\"coords\"]\n  69:             patch[coords[:, 0] - pr0, coords[:, 1] - pc0] = source\n  70:             enclosed = object_interior(patch, background=bg)\n  71:             fill_mask = (patch == bg) & ~enclosed\n  72: \n  73:             input_region = g[pr0 : pr1 + 1, pc0 : pc1 + 1]\n  74:             target = out[pr0 : pr1 + 1, pc0 : pc1 + 1]\n  75:             assert np.all(input_region[fill_mask] == bg), \"halo cells must be background in the input\"\n  76:             assert np.all((target[fill_mask] == bg) | (target[fill_mask] == halo)), \"halo regions must not conflict\"\n  77:             target[fill_mask] = halo\n  78: \n  79:     return out\n  80: \n  81: # --------------- generator (att1, Claude) ---------------\n  82: \n  83: # Canvas and color palette.\n  84: BACKGROUND = 0\n  85: GRID_SIZE_MIN, GRID_SIZE_MAX = 20, 26\n  86: AVAILABLE_COLORS = tuple(range(1, 10))\n  87: \n  88: # Legend layout.\n  89: LEGEND_SOURCE_COL, LEGEND_HALO_COL = 0, 1\n  90: LEGEND_MARKER_SIZE, LEGEND_VERTICAL_GAP = 2, 3\n  91: \n  92: # Sprite geometry and acceptance.\n  93: SPRITE_SIZE, SPRITE_MIRROR_COLS = 5, 3\n  94: SPRITE_AREA_MIN, SPRITE_AREA_MAX = 12, 16\n  95: SPRITE_FILL_JITTER, MAX_INTERIOR_HOLES = 0.05, 3\n  96: SPRITE_RETRIES = 300\n  97: \n  98: # Object counts and placement.\n  99: INSTANCE_MIN, INSTANCE_MAX = 1, 3\n 100: OBJECT_MIN_COL, EDGE_CLIP_MARGIN = 2, 1\n 101: EDGE_CLIP_PROB, HALO_PADDING = 0.1, 1\n 102: PLACEMENT_RETRIES = 200\n 103: \n 104: # Whole-sample retry budget.\n 105: GENERATION_RETRIES = 300\n 106: \n 107: \n-- generate_input at line 260 --\n 260: def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n 261:     for _ in range(GENERATION_RETRIES):\n 262:         result = _gen_attempt()\n 263:         if result is not None:\n 264:             return result\n 265:     raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate valid input after {GENERATION_RETRIES} attempts\")\n\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py =====\n-- main at line 7 --\n   7: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n   8:     \"\"\"\n   9:     Slide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n  10: \n  11:     Input structure:\n  12:     - bg = most common color.\n  13:     - Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n  14:     - Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n  15: \n  16:     Rule:\n  17:     - Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n  18:     - Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n  19:     - Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row =